rian  thanks brian ross coming up on law on  crime daily can a convicted killer scott  peterson get a new trial how alleged  jeremy's conor can possibly free this  charged murderer plus new court  documents released for another man  accused of killing his wife what this  lengthy affidavit reveals about barry  murphy's marriage  [Music]  welcome back to law and crime daily  newly released court documents bring  shocking new details to light against  accused wife killer barry morphew  a 131-page affidavit paints a colorful  picture of the life barry and suzanne  morphy shared more than a dozen times  morphew denied knowledge of his wife's  affair even calling his marriage quote  perfect but text message between the  couple shows a different story as  suzanne tech said quote i'm done and  let's handle this civilly the affidavit  also points to the recording of a march  2020 fight between the pair after  suzanne confided in her friend that  barry quote won't speak a divorce the  friend bought suzanne a spy pen to  record their conversations suzanne moore  if you went missing on mother's day 2020  in a video barry morview claimed she was  kidnapped and begged for her return  authorities say it was an act and that  he actually killed her last week a judge  ruled that murphy's trial will begin on  may 3rd of next year he faces several  charges including first degree murder  and tampering with a deceased human body  back for their take on morpheus  affidavit is criminal defense attorney  bernardo violone and co-host terry  austin terry lawn crimes adams class  fell wrote an article on the website  that i recommend to everyone actually  check out listing five of the most  eye-popping facts in this 131 page  affidavit which one shocked you the most  well they all shocked me brian actually  but the internet searches i think were  the ones that were a bit disturbing he  does admit that he looked at porn but  he's saying he never went on any dating  sites and some of the sites they found  were about younger girls finding a cute  girl near selita and he said that he  never did that and he denied that he  ever had any problems with his marriage  and frankly it would have been better  for him i think to admit to some of the  problems particularly since they've come  up on his phone and to just say he yes  looked at some porn and maybe they were  pop-ups and he did mention that but i  think he should have just  confronted them and told him the truth  about everything on his phone yeah  that's not too incriminating in terms of  what he's being charged with considering  all the other facts bernardo what do  these text messages give the prosecution  if his case goes to trial  well what the text messages gives the  prosecution is motive motive motive  motive pure motive prosecutor doesn't  have to prove motive however motive does  help a jury try to decide why is it that  this person killed this other person and  here you have motive  as well as you're discrediting him with  him saying that he did not know that she  was cheating on him  absolutely it makes sense and they'll  definitely intertwine that in terms of  the story telling if this case does go  to trial that along with some of the  little lies that he's making i think  will make for a strong prosecution case  thank you both and in florida a murder  suspect has officially pled not guilty  to all charges  brian potter was found murdered outside  of his apartment in kendall florida back  in november 2006. former teammate  rashawn jones has long been suspected  but was never arrested due to a lack of  evidence fifteen years later cell phone  data placed jones in the area the day  and time of the murder patta and jones  were involved in confrontations before  the shot was fired on friday prosecutors  formally filed second-degree murder  charges against jones with the  possibility of upgrading to first degree  once the prosecutors present the case to  a grand jury  when we come back the next steps for  scott peterson the shocking new filings  by his defense why the convicted wife  killer's attorneys are asking for  immunity for the one of the jurors who  decided his fate next  [Music]  welcome back an update now on the story  law and crime daily has been following  for months the trial before the man  accused of killing his wife children and  dog has been pushed back  the trial of anthony dot was originally  set to begin on monday but prosecutors  requested more time to sift through  large amounts of evidence prosecutors  say todd murdered his wife three kids  and the family dog in january 2020. the  family was renting a home in celebration  florida originally developed by the walt  disney company and nearby the orlando  theme parks in december 2019 the rental  homes landlord attempted to evict the  family after nearly five thousand  dollars in rent money went unpaid while  todd originally confessed to the murders  he later suggested his wife was  responsible todd has entered a plea of  not guilty he is due back in court on  october 18th  and jury selection is underway in  arizona for mark gooch a u.s airman who  is accused of murdering sasha krauss a  mennonite schoolteacher krauss  disappeared from her community in  january 2020 her remains were found over  a month later an autopsy revealed she  died of blunt force trauma and a gunshot  wound to the head cell phone records and  surveillance video put gooch at the  scene of the crime previous cell phone  records obtained show that gooch and his  brothers show contempt towards those in  the mennonite faith mark gooch is  charged with first-degree murder and  faces life in prison if found guilty  turning now to the west coast where  scott peterson is set to appear in court  on wednesday as his defense team inches  closer to the promise of a new trial  peterson was convicted of the 2002 death  of his wife lacey and their unborn son  connor in 2005 he was given the death  penalty but the sentence was later  reduced to life without parole after the  california supreme court cited  significant errors in the jury selection  it's on those grounds of germ misconduct  that peterson's defense teams say the  court owes him a new trial juror number  seven later revealed to be richelle  niece failed to disclose domestic abuse  in her past peterson's defense argued  nice purposely tried to convict him  because of her past issues court  documents show peterson's defense saying  nice will plead the fifth amendment at  wednesday's evidentiary hearing unless  granted immunity from the prosecution  after an appearance last month one of  peterson's defense attorneys addressed  the dispute with juror 7.  i think it is somewhat  difficult  for us to understand why they don't want  the truth to come out  we find it very difficult to understand  why they wouldn't as  prosecutors and as  their role to do justice why they  wouldn't want the information about  rochelle nice to come out  we are perplexed by that and don't  understand why it is that they continue  to fight this when all we are trying to  do is get it the truth  now bernardo it's absolutely the  prosecution decision to give or not give  nice immunity but why wouldn't they in a  case this serious for several reasons  one you do not want to reward bad  behavior you don't want a reward of  giving immunity to a potential juror  that lied under oath because that's  technically what you're doing when  you're giving her immunity but two is  like what can she reveal she can reveal  some information that can cause for a  new trial to be ordered in this case and  the prosecution probably doesn't want to  go through a trial all over again which  is sad because in the end it's like  because you were a victim  does not mean that you're not qualified  to be a juror what would have happened  is that there would have been further  inquiry to see whether you can still  remain fair and impartial absolutely now  terry with nina saying she plead the  fifth where do you think that would  leave peterson in terms of getting a new  trial  well it all depends on how the judge  looks at that information frankly the  fact that she's pleading the fifth means  that she's hiding something and what is  she hiding the fact that she may have  understood the questions that were being  asked during voir dire and that she  intentionally kept that information from  the attorneys and from the court so i  think that the judge when this hearing  this evidentiary hearing does occur is  going to have to figure out what else we  need in terms of information do we talk  to other jurors how are we going to find  out what in fact this juror knew or the  judge could very well hold it against  her and come to the conclusion that she  in fact was withholding information and  in fact peterson is deserving of a new  trial so much depends on what happens at  that hearing yeah it's all about  interpretation at that point we'll see  how that plays out as we continue to  follow this case thank you both and  thank you for joining us here on law and  crime daily we'll see you next time as  we discuss justice in america  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

